course: dictionary
lesson: 10836
title: Verbal: spoken, not done
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Verbal means using words, usually spoken out loud rather than written down or acted out. A «verbal warning» is one a referee gives by saying it, so the player hears it.
explain: «Solid revenge was what he wanted, not cheap, windy, verbal triumphs.» The clues are «solid» and «cheap, windy». Here verbal triumphs are wins made only with words, with nothing real done. Look at the next part: he jumps on a horse and gallops off, which is a real action, not just talk.
explain: Verbal also means to do with words, as in «verbal reasoning», a word puzzle test used in UK schools. A «verbal agreement» is a deal made by speaking, with nothing written down. Ask yourself: is this said, or is it written or done?
fact: Verbal means in words, and usually spoken.
fact: «Verbal» can also mean to do with words or language, as in «verbal skills».
fact: «Verbal reasoning» is a type of word puzzle test used in UK schools.
fact: «A verbal dispute» is an argument carried on in words, not with action.
fact: In grammar, «verbal» means to do with verbs.
exercise: «Arguing about a word nobody has defined is a verbal dispute.» What is a verbal dispute? | A fight with fists | A written letter of complaint | A quiet game of chess | *An argument carried on in words
exercise: A referee gives a player a «verbal warning». What is the warning? | A letter sent home to parents | *Something said out loud | A painted sign on the pitch | Something done with a ball
exercise: What is a «verbal agreement»? | A contract signed in ink | A bet placed with coins | A drawing of a handshake | *A deal made by speaking, with nothing written
test: «Solid revenge was what he wanted, not cheap, windy, verbal triumphs.» Why does the author call the triumphs cheap and windy? | They were long and hard to win | They cost a lot of money | They were kind and generous | *They are only wins in words, with nothing real done
test: What are «verbal skills»? | *Skills with words and language | Skills with paint and brushes | Skills with a ball and bat | Skills with numbers and sums
exam: Which phrase means words said out loud, not written down? | a silent wave | a written report | *a verbal warning | a painted picture
